<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/ice-storm.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/ice-storm-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/ice-storm.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="627" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/property-appraisals/">Services</a><span>/</span>Ice Storm &amp; Snow Load Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker fade-up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.08s">Ice Storm and Snow Load Damage Appraisal in Oregon</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.15s">The February 2021 and January 2024 ice storms showed how quickly freezing rain turns the Willamette Valley’s trees into hazards. Winter claims combine roof, tree, plumbing and interior damage, and they are often underpaid.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-w" href="/reach-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts fade-up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Big events</small><b>Feb 2021 · Jan 2024 ice storms</b></div><div><small>Damage</small><b>Limbs · Gutters · Pipes · Roofs</b></div><div><small>Regions</small><b>Valley · Gorge · Cascades</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why Oregon winters produce complicated claims</h2>
<p>Western Oregon doesn’t see much snow or ice in a typical year, so homes and trees aren’t built for it. When freezing rain coats Douglas firs and big-leaf maples, limbs come down on roofs, cars, fences and power lines. In February 2021, an ice storm left hundreds of thousands of people in the Portland and Salem areas without power, some for more than a week. Another ice storm in January 2024 hit the Portland metro area and the Columbia River Gorge hard.</p>
<p>Higher elevations and Central Oregon deal with snow load. Bend, Sisters and towns along the Cascades see heavy snow that can overload decks, carports and low-slope roofs.</p>
<h2>What these claims typically include</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tree and limb impacts</strong> on roofs, siding, gutters and outbuildings</li>
<li><strong>Ice dam water intrusion</strong> into ceilings and walls</li>
<li><strong>Frozen and burst pipes</strong>, especially in crawlspaces and exterior walls, during long outages</li>
<li><strong>Snow-load damage</strong> to carports, patio covers, decks and metal buildings</li>
<li><strong>Food spoilage and additional living expenses</strong> when the power is out for days, where the policy provides them</li>
</ul>
<h2>Common disputes</h2>
<p>Insurers often price a fallen-limb claim as a small roof patch, without looking for cracked rafters, damaged decking or water that soaked insulation. Pipe-burst claims are sometimes limited to the plumbing repair, leaving out drying, demolition and rebuilding. Snow-load claims raise questions about whether a structure was overloaded by the storm or failed from age. We document each of these carefully.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Outage losses</b>Some policies limit coverage for losses caused by off-premises power failure. Read the wording or send it to us before assuming spoiled food or frozen pipes are covered.</div>
<h2>After the storm</h2>
<p>Take photos before tree removal, keep invoices for emergency services, and note how long the power was out. Those details make a winter claim much easier to value.</p>

<h2>Questions about ice storm &amp; snow load appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is tree removal covered after an ice storm?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many homeowner policies pay a limited amount to remove a tree that falls on a covered structure. Trees that fall without hitting anything are often not covered. Limits vary, so check your form.</p></div></details><details><summary>My pipes froze while the power was out. Is that covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often, if you took reasonable steps to keep heat on or drain the system. Some policies have specific conditions for vacant homes or off-premises power failure. We review the form first.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can snow-load damage be appraised?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, if the insurer accepts the claim and disputes the amount. If it denies the claim as wear or poor construction, that is a coverage issue.</p></div></details></div>
